AB  - Log anomaly detection is pivotal for ensuring the reliability and security of modern network and data infrastructures, serving as a cornerstone of Artificial Intelligence for IT Operations (AIOps). However, conventional single-model approaches often exhibit limited accuracy and poor generalization when confronted with the intricate, diverse patterns inherent in real-world system logs. Moreover, most existing methods lack effective feedback mechanisms, hindering continuous performance improvement. To address these challenges, we propose LogPDGMoE, a novel log anomaly detection framework built upon a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture augmented with a Parallel Dual-noise Gating (PDG) network. The innovation of PDG lies in its dual learnable projections derived from feature representations, which independently condition the injected Gaussian noise to enable self-optimized routing. This design effectively mitigates expert load imbalance and adapts to dynamic feature drift prevalent in log streams. Additionally, a weighted loss function is introduced to harmoniously balance exploration and exploitation during training. Extensive evaluation on three public benchmarks demonstrates that LogPDGMoE achieves highly competitive F1-scores of 97.11%, 99.17%, and 99.39%, respectively. Compared with leading baselines, our approach delivers superior detection accuracy, stronger cross-system generalization across heterogeneous log distributions, and enhanced robustness, establishing LogPDGMoE as a highly efficient, scalable, and adaptive solution for large-scale cyber environments.
