KW  - Distributed systems
KW  - Large language models
AB  - Modern distributed storage systems comprise thousands of interdependent components, where fault diagnosis requires simultaneously understanding statistical correlations among events and semantic plausibility. Existing approaches either rely on symbolic reasoning and struggle with rare faults, or leverage neural networks but lack causal constraints and are prone to spurious inferences; moreover, they generally cannot assess their own prediction quality or quantify prediction uncertainty. This paper proposes NeuSymRCA, a framework that achieves structured neuro-symbolic integration through dual-path collaborative reasoning. The symbolic path encodes historical statistical patterns, while the neural path invokes large language models to evaluate semantic plausibility. The two paths are dynamically fused through a reliability-aware log-linear pooling mechanism. The core innovation lies in embedding reliability quantification into the reasoning process, enabling fusion weights to adaptively adjust according to different scenarios, and achieving few-shot learning through knowledge transfer by extracting general causal priors from language models. Evaluation on the Train Ticket microservice benchmark, with supplementary validation on the AIOps Challenge 2020 public dataset, demonstrates that the framework achieves an overall accuracy of 82.4%, with a 12 percentage point improvement over the best baseline on rare faults.
